infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title The one axis: time-ordered vs random IDs
  desc Both mint globally-unique IDs with no coordination — they split on sortability, and pay opposite costs
  compares
    - label Time-ordered (Snowflake, UUIDv7)
      children
        - label Timestamp in the high bits
          desc Snowflake 41-bit ms epoch; UUIDv7 48-bit Unix-ms — IDs sort by creation
        - label Right-edge index inserts
          desc Mostly sequential keys keep B-tree locality, like auto-increment had
        - label Range-scannable by time
          desc You can scan a time window straight off the primary key
        - label Leaks time + trusts the clock
          desc Two IDs reveal when each was made; a backward clock step risks duplicates
    - label Random (UUIDv4)
      children
        - label 122 random bits, no timestamp
          desc Pure entropy in a 128-bit value — collisions negligible, zero shared state
        - label Fragments the B-tree
          desc Each insert lands at a random leaf: page splits, lost cache locality
        - label Can't range-scan
          desc No ordering to exploit; and a clustered table physically fragments
        - label Leaks nothing, unguessable
          desc Best for public IDs — reset tokens, public object slugs
theme
  palette #14b8a6 #f43f5e
